>      * Real-VGA timings we approximate:
>      *   - Frame period ~14.3 ms (70 Hz, the original VGA mode-3 / mode-13h rate).
>      *   - Vertical sync pulse ~64 us (well under a millisecond).
>      *   - The whole vertical-blank interval is ~1.4 ms, during which DE is low.
>      *   - DE is high for the rest of the frame, but real hardware toggles it
>      *     line-by-line at ~31.5 kHz, so guests doing many reads per "line"
>      *     see a fast oscillation -- we mimic that by toggling DE every read
>      *     while inside the active-scan portion of the frame.
3499,3506c3582,3590
<      * We do this with a 4 state machine. Each state has a count associated
<      * with it to represent the relative time spent in each state. When this
<      * count is exhausted the machine moves into the next state. One Inb
<      * equals 1 count. The states are as follows:
<      *     0: VS low, DE high.
<      *     1: VS low, DE toggles. This works via an internal state.
<      *     3: VS low, DE high.
<      *     4: VS high,DE high.
---
>      * The previous implementation in this file ran the state machine off a
>      * per-call counter (the same 40-read cycle regardless of wall-clock
>      * speed).  That worked when the guest's poll rate happened to match
>      * VGA timings; with the WHP backend (~60 us per IO exit) poll rate
>      * drops to ~15 kHz, and guests calibrating CPU speed against "IN AL,
>      * 3DAh between two vsync edges" measure wildly wrong numbers, causing
>      * every tick-paced animation/timing to run slow.  Driving the state
>      * off wall clock makes the calibration land on real-hardware values
>      * regardless of host backend.
